 "acknowledgments": "",
 "limitations": "### Appendix C Limitations and Future Work\n\nOur prompts, executor profiles, and evaluation benchmarks are predominantly English. The current results therefore do not establish whether the learned orchestration policy transfers to multilingual tasks, code-switched inputs, or language-specific tools and executors. Multilingual settings may require the orchestrator to account explicitly for language proficiency and cross-lingual information integration.\n\nThe current evaluation further considers a limited number of executor configurations and assumes that the supplied capability and cost profiles are accurate and stable. In practice, executor behavior may vary across domains, prompts, and deployment conditions, while larger agent pools may substantially increase the orchestration search space. Evaluating robustness to noisy or evolving profiles and scaling to larger, more heterogeneous executor pools remain important directions for future work.\n\nFinally, tree-structured rollouts increase training-time computation. The number of candidate trajectories grows with the number of initial trajectories ( B ), branches per expansion ( N ), and expansion rounds ( E ). Selective branching, early pruning, and trajectory reuse may reduce this training overhead.\n\n##",
